# NOTE: gitleaks 8.30.1 matches a `.gitleaksignore` entry against the finding's
# *internal* fingerprint `commit:file:rule-id:START-LINE` (see detect.go
# `AddFinding`): the 4th field is the integer start line at the commit that
# introduced the match. `--redact` masks only the secret *value* (the report's
# `Secret`/`Match` become `REDACTED` and the reported `Fingerprint`'s 4th field
# is shown as `***`); the top-level `StartLine` integer is preserved, so the
# regen command below still emits the correct start line WITH redaction.
#
# gitleaks false positives — no real credentials in this repo.
#
# Every entry below is a `generic-api-key` hit on an explicitly-fake test
# fixture, a documentation example, or a code identifier. gitleaks docs:
# https://github.com/gitleaks/gitleaks#gitleaksignore
#
# Fingerprints are scoped to the exact commit that introduced the match, so a
# *new* secret with the same file/rule (later commit, or different line) is
# still reported. Format is `commit:file:rule-id:start-line` with the full
# 40-char commit SHA (abbreviated SHAs do NOT match). Regenerate with:
#
#   gitleaks git --redact --report-format json --report-path report.json .
#   jq -r '.[] | "\(.Commit):\(.File):\(.RuleID):\(.StartLine)"' report.json
#
# When adding a new fixture secret, prefer a `# gitleaks:allow` comment on the
# line itself; only add a fingerprint here for matches buried in history.

